AB  - Small, irregularly shaped water bodies such as reservoirs, lakes, and rivers are crucial freshwater sources. Accurate monitoring of their surrounding environments is essential for effective water resource protection. Satellite remote sensing technology provides large-scale, high-accuracy, and high-resolution data for these areas. However, complex surroundings, shadows from mountains, buildings, clouds, and algae overgrowth reduce classification accuracy. Image fusion technology can address this by combining data from different satellite sensors, overcoming the limitations of high spatial and spectral resolution. Biucubic, Brovey, Principal Components Analysis (PCA), Smoothing Filter-based Intensity Modulation (SFIM), Gram-Schmidt (GS), modulation transfer function-generalized Laplacian pyramid (MTF_GLP), Adaptive Gram-Schmid (GSA), Pansharpening by Convolutional Neural Networks (PCNN), and PanNet were selected for evaluation the performance of fusion. The spectral retention and spatial resolution enhancement capabilities of these methods were assessed using metrics such as Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR), Structural SIMilarity (SSIM), Spectral Angle Mapper(SAM), Erreur Relative Globale Adimensionnelle de Synthése Spatial Correlation Coefficient(ERAGS), and Spatial Correlation Coefficient(SCC). The results show that (1) the PCNN method is the most effective for the pixel-level fusion of GF2 and Sentinel-2 images. It is found that MTF_GLP method has better spectral preserving ability (PSNR=17.559), and the method based on component replacement (PCA GS GSA) can effectively preserve the spatial structure of the image. Overall, the comprehensive performance of the PCNN method is the best (PSNR =17.558, SSIM =0.860, SAM =0.301, ERAGS =2 2. 2 2 0, SCC=0.910). (2)In the task of classifying ground objects around the water body, fusion images can effectively improve the resolution of ground objects (1 m) and the classification accuracy is higher. Applied with the Random Forest method, the fusion image improves the classification accuracy from 24.98 % to 9 3. 9 9 % of panchromatic image. Particularly, the fusion image performs better in identifying dams and roads around reservoirs. It was indicated that fusion images can solve the mixed pixel decomposition problem of multi-spectral images to a certain extent. The study shows that with an appropriate fusion method, images from different satellites can be effectively fused. The fusion images with high spectral and spatial resolution are efficient in classifying land use types around water bodies such as reservoirs and also in acquiring information of the aquatic environment.
